SM(199,{"n":"Philippians - Introduction","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/Philippians - Introduction.mp3","d":2201.0,"s":[[0.24,"Started. And tonight we're uh starting a new book, uh, the book of Philippians, and so most of tonight will be my long rambly uh introduction to uh the book of Corinthians, or not Corinthians, Philippians."],[20.4,"Confused everyone. Uh Philippians."],[23.76,"Um we just finished Ephesians."],[29.76,"Philippians is a book that's a little different uh in some ways than the other epistles of Saint Paul that uh we've been reading."],[42.72,"Um so there's some there's some basics that are the same."],[46.58,"So St. Paul wrote it."],[48.87,"So, and we talked uh all the way back in the introduction to the book, St."],[53.43,"Paul's epistle to the Romans."],[54.99,"Uh, the introduction to Romans, we talked about uh what it means that Saint Paul uh wrote it."],[62.44,"And we'll reiterate that uh here that when we're saying Saint Paul wrote these these epistles, or that it's St."],[71.32,"Paul's epistle to blank, we aren't saying that Saint Paul sat down with a pen and a papyrus or a pen and a piece of vellum and wrote it by hand."],[83.5,"That is the case for zero of Saint Paul's epistles."],[87.82,"Uh, we know that for a number of reasons, including the fact that in a few of them, Saint Paul will say near the end, for example, see with what large letters I write to you in my own hand."],[102.68,"Which means the rest of it was not."],[105.74,"So he's he'll he'll affix a little personal note or signature, you know, at the end uh of some of them."],[114.22,"So that's not the case for any of them."],[117.9,"What is the case is that Saint Paul uh was involved in the writing of all of them."],[124.7,"So on one end of, it's sort of a spectrum, and on one end of that spectrum, you have um, for example, uh St."],[135.98,"Paul's Epistle to the Galatians, St."],[137.66,"Paul's epistle to the Romans would be good examples."],[141.18,"First Corinthians, of the ones we've read."],[143.66,"Those are all good examples."],[145.3,"Where Saint Paul dictated it to what was called in Greek an emanuensis, sort of like a secretary, a scribe, uh professional scribe, where uh the process was he would dictate the letter."],[162.58,"The scribe would write physically write it, but also sort of make little grammar corrections."],[171.64,"Polish it up a little for written instead of spoken, you know what I mean?"],[174.84,"Like taking dictation for a letter."],[177.25,"But clean it up a little bit."],[179.33,"Once he had finished doing that, St."],[181.65,"Paul would have taken it and read it."],[185.47,"And if he felt like there were any corrections he needed to make, oh, I I didn't mean that I meant, you know, this, I didn't mean that word."],[190.91,"He would make whatever changes"],[191.59,"I meant this word or whatever."],[196.05,"The scribe would finalize it."],[198.53,"And then usually that same person, that same scribe, would be the one who would take the letter to its recipient or recipients."],[207.41,"And would publicly read it."],[209.81,"Because of course, not everyone was literate, so it's they didn't have a way to quickly and easily make copies, so they didn't pass it out at church or something."],[218.39,"Or put it in the bulletin."],[220.87,"He would uh read it out loud publicly, and he was also then expected to be able to answer any questions that people had about it."],[230.79,"Um, so these letters were first publicly read in the churches, right from the get-go."],[235.03,"Now we're taking St"],[236.51,"That's what they were."],[237.55,"So us reading them in a liturgy is not like a new thing."],[242.59,"Paul's letters and reading them out in liturgy."],[244.99,"That's what was originally done with them, is that they were read out uh in the churches uh that received them."],[252.59,"So that's sort of one end of the spectrum."],[254.99,"And I say it's one end of the spectrum because that's the spectrum is uh how many how many factors there are between the person Saint Paul and the finished product."],[269.97,"So on one end of the spectrum, you have Saint Paul writing it through a scribe and approving the text."],[278.06,"So that's that's one end of the spectrum."],[279.58,"Paul's Epistle to the Philippians is sort of in the middle"],[281.06,"An epistle like St."],[285.54,"Why do I say it's in the middle?"],[288.1,"Because he has a co-author."],[292.1,"And that's already in verse one of chapter one of St."],[297.62,"Paul's Epistle to the Philippians."],[299.06,"You'll notice it says Paul and Timothy, bondservants, plural of Jesus Christ, is who's writing."],[307.46,"So he's listing Saint Timothy as his co-author."],[312.42,"So that means there's at least one more person involved."],[316.1,"Now there's St."],[317.06,"Paul, there's St."],[318.18,"Timothy, there's a scribe."],[321.32,"All involved in producing this letter."],[323.8,"Paul's a little, it's a little less direct"],[324.16,"So St. Paul's Epistle to the Hebrews."],[337.42,"Where exactly how much involvement Saint Paul had isn't totally clear."],[347.5,"It looks. St."],[349.18,"Paul's Epistle to the Hebrews, if you just look at the Greek of it, the Greek style and everything, it looks a lot more like St."],[355.58,"Luke than St."],[356.94,"Paul. And so there are a number of theories in terms of how that worked."],[362.48,"Whether St. Luke was sort of recording a sermon that St."],[367.44,"Paul gave, because there are certain features, and we'll talk about this more when we get to Hebrews."],[373.0,"But there's certain features of it that make it look kind of like a sermon or a homily more than a letter in terms of its format."],[380.12,"Um, so it may be that Saint Luke was recording a uh sermon that Saint Paul gave and sort of send then sending it."],[389.56,"So he attached the letter stuff because he was sending it out, to to a church."],[396.3,"Um but there are other theories, but that's sort of the the far end."],[401.03,"Where it doesn't seem like Saint Paul was directly involved with the text, but that what's there in the text sort of came from Saint Paul's mind."],[411.77,"But there's there's some distance there between him and the text."],[417.53,"So that's sort of the range."],[420.67,"And it's it's important."],[422.11,"The reason I'm going through this again is that there are all kinds of debates, because of course people need to publish journal articles and people need to write dissertations about exactly which of St."],[435.39,"Paul's epistles he wrote."],[438.61,"And so if you pick up any book about the New Testament, they'll say, well, St."],[442.13,"Paul didn't definitely didn't write Hebrews, probably didn't write a bunch of his other epistles, and then definitely wrote."],[450.29,"Usually there's about seven that they'll say he definitely wrote."],[454.77,"Those seven are the ones that are over here with Romans and Galatians on the one end of the spectrum."],[460.91,"And then Hebrews definitely not on the other end."],[464.03,"And then the other ones debatably in between."],[468.4,"But if you take into account the fact that most of the ones that are quote unquote debatable have a listed co-author."],[477.7,"And you take into account that Hebrews, for example, is written in the style of one of St."],[482.58,"Paul's companions. It becomes pretty clear why they don't all look alike."],[490.89,"In terms of the Greek."],[492.97,"And it becomes pretty clear why the tr the church, nonetheless, who was reading it in the original Greek, and were even more aware of the differences in style than we are."],[503.91,"Still had this tradition that these all come from Saint Paul."],[509.83,"So that's why I think it's kind of fruitless, again, unless you just absolutely have to publish a dissertation."],[518.28,"Or write a dissertation, and this is the only topic you could come up with."],[521.96,"I I think it's kind of fruitless to sit and argue about, you know, did St."],[528.68,"Paul write them?"],[529.4,"Well, no, he didn't sit down and write any of it."],[532.1,"But it has clearly has some connection to St."],[535.38,"Paul. The ideas are quote unquote Pauline."],[539.16,"And so trying to sort of hash these things out is kind of irrelevant to interpreting them and is irrelevant to the tradition of the church."],[549.7,"That says that this body of literature reflects the mind of Saint Paul."],[556.28,"This is the gospel as Saint Paul proclaimed it."],[561.33,"And none of it was put to paper by him."],[563.81,"And that's fine."],[565.01,"No one ever claimed it was."],[567.97,"So this is uh St."],[569.73,"Paul's epistle to the Philippians that we're gonna be uh reading now, is there in the middle."],[576.07,"So St. Timothy's involved."],[578.55,"And uh these uh epistles. where Saint Timothy is listed as a co-author all date from around the same period"],[592.27,"And it's there's there's good argument, shall we say, that uh they actually originate from Ephesus."],[603.47,"Saint Paul alludes to some trouble he had in Ephesus."],[607.23,"Ephesus was a major center."],[609.31,"Saint Timothy, as far as we can tell, until St."],[612.11,"Paul sent him to Crete, was in Ephesus."],[616.91,"And so it is very likely that, like Ephesians that we just read, that Philippians was written sometime in probably the mid 50s A.D."],[629.15,"and And was written from Ephesus while he was in prison, that's part of why we have a co-author."],[636.23,"It's part of why we have another person involved, because St."],[639.03,"Paul was less free to compose as he usually composed."],[643.19,"And so St."],[643.83,"Timothy was involved in assisting him there."],[650.55,"The older theory, and this may be reflected."],[655.91,"In fact, I'm almost willing to bet it was."],[658.55,"Yes. Okay. So the Orthodox Study Bible on the uh frontispiece has the older theory that says that St."],[667.99,"Paul wrote this while he was imprisoned in Rome."],[671.91,"There's a bunch of problems with that."],[674.15,"The biggest one being that there's nothing to indicate that St."],[678.11,"Timothy ever went to Rome."],[681.07,"Seems pretty clear he stayed at Ephesus, and that's why Saint Paul had to write 1st and 2 Timothy to Timothy from Rome."],[690.93,"If St. Timothy was there with him, he wouldn't have had to send him letters because he was at Ephesus."],[698.55,"Like that seems pretty obvious."],[700.39,"Um and uh so that's that's a biggie."],[705.35,"Um St. Paul also mentions things, as we'll see, especially when we get to the end of St."],[713.19,"Paul's epistle to the Philippians."],[714.79,"He mentions things he's planning to do that don't make sense. if it's when he was imprisoned in Rome"],[724.73,"Because the things he's saying he's planning to do are things he did before that."],[730.53,"So that doesn't really work."],[732.93,"So it it's much more likely that that uh this is written during that same imprisonment in Ephesus, which was temporary, uh, and uh therefore this, is one of his earlier epistles."],[752.29,"Now, when I say earlier, all of St."],[754.13,"Paul's epistles were written between probably about 45 and then uh the 60s when he was martyred by Nero."],[762.61,"This is about 20 years when he writes all of these"],[763.93,"So this isn't a huge span of time."],[768.57,"But so this would be sort of smack in the middle."],[771.71,"Sort of smack in the middle of his ministry and his writing career."],[779.15,"So he's writing it."],[780.59,"We talked about how well you've had we've had epistles like Romans and 1st and 2nd Corinthians, uh, where uh St."],[789.95,"Paul wrote it to a particular community in a particular city."],[793.31,"And then we've also had like Galatians and Ephesians, where they were sort of more circular."],[798.37,"Galatia, remember, is a region, so it was to all the churches there."],[802.61,"We talked about how St."],[803.81,"Paul's epistle to the Ephesians was actually written from Ephesus, probably around this same time period, and circulated in all the churches around the Christian communities around Ephesus."],[817.21,"So this one, St."],[819.05,"Paul's Epistle to the Philippians, is going to a particular city, a particular Philippi."],[824.49,"There were a bunch of Philippis."],[826.01,"There was a Caesarea Philippi."],[827.61,"There were a bunch of Philippians."],[829.29,"But this is going to one particular one in uh Massiv in the Roman province of Macedonia."],[837.53,"That's uh which had also been part of um of uh Alexander the Great's Macedonia, because of course he was Macedonian."],[849.93,"Um a little bit of history of that city, um, because I know, you know, we're not huge fans of Philippi, probably."],[865.01,"I haven't done a ton of research into Philippi in particular, but uh Philippi is a city that was first sort of incorporated as a Greek city state by Philip of Macedon, by Alexander the Great's father."],[879.49,"And he named it after himself in a fit of humility."],[883.41,"Um his son did that a lot too, named cities after himself."],[887.65,"So he named it Philippi."],[889.89,"There were already people settled there."],[892.49,"Uh, but the reason that Philip um made it into a city state is that there were gold and silver mines very close to it."],[902.49,"And so extracting that became important to him, and turning Philippi into a city gave him the infrastructure to do that."],[912.41,"Um, and it was also near the border of his territory."],[917.05,"That territory had been recently annexed by him, uh, was on the border with Thrace."],[922.65,"Um, so Philippi had this sort of Greek start."],[928.33,"Uh, it came to much more prominence later in the Roman period."],[933.45,"Uh, particularly in uh 42 BC."],[938.17,"This is where uh the armies of Octavian, who would later be Caesar Augustus, and Mark Antony, back when they were friends, met up with finally tracked down Cassius and Brutus, who had killed Caesar."],[954.05,"And their sort of final showdown battle was near Philippi."],[960.53,"And after that happened, after that battle, and Mark Antony and Octavian were victorious as a reward to their veterans who had fought in that battle."],[975.25,"This is how the Roman system worked."],[977.33,"When you were a veteran, what you received at the end of your military service was land."],[982.73,"They would give uh Roman veterans received tracts of land usually at the edges of Roman territory, so that Romans would go and settle there and sort of Romanize wherever it was and bring the Roman way of life."],[998.73,"That helped them solidify their empire."],[1001.51,"Because the natives weren't necessarily Roman, weren't necessarily fond of Rome, but we bring in uh Roman settlers."],[1011.43,"And so that began then."],[1013.91,"A number of uh veterans were rewarded with territory uh uh with land around Philippi and went and settled there."],[1021.11,"And then after uh the Battle of Actium, when Octavian and Marc Antony had turned on each other and had their final showdown, the newly minted Caesar Augustus settled another wave of his veterans who had been loyal to him in bringing him to power in the area around Philippi, and he also um in another uh fit of humility changed the name of the city."],[1045.79,"So the city is referred to in the Bible as Philippi, and we call this Philippians, but technically the name of the uh city was uh uh uh Iulio Augusto Philippi."],[1060.48,"So he named it after Julius Caesar, his illustrious ancestor, himself, Augustus, to the day he had just taken, and then kept uh Philip of Macedon's uh name on the end."],[1072.64,"Um but so what this meant was that uh Philippi was a very Roman city."],[1081.32,"When I say that, for example, most of the cities we've talked about, in Asia Minor, in Greece, you know, obviously in Palestine and Syria, like Antioch, um, even in Egypt, um, from within the Roman Empire, the inscriptions and everything are in Greek."],[1102.79,"The documents we have from there are in Greek, because Greek was the lingua franca, Greek was the trade language that sort of everybody knew."],[1111.59,"Almost all of the inscriptions we found in Philippi from the first century are in Latin, not Greek."],[1119.27,"And only Romans knew Latin."],[1119.99,"Sort of everybody knew Greek, but only Romans knew Latin"],[1125.37,"So that tells you the percentage of the population."],[1130.59,"That that was that was uh Roman."],[1134.03,"Umciously Roman. Yes, sir."],[1138.03,"Where would Philippi fall today?"],[1141.31,"And in is it in Turkey, Bulgaria, it's in Greece."],[1145.31,"It's in the northeastern part of Greece."],[1146.91,"Okay. Yeah. Yeah."],[1148.51,"What what's called Macedonia today is not ancient Macedonia."],[1156.19,"And Greeks are very particular about that."],[1158.59,"About pointing that out."],[1159.79,"Ancient Macedonia is part of Greece."],[1162.43,"Yeah, so it's in it's in uh the northeastern part of Greece now"],[1162.91,"Um but so vi it was it was very, very Roman."],[1174.23,"Uh and so there was a very strict class system."],[1181.19,"Um where the Romans the Roman citizens, these Roman settlers, these Roman veterans were the movers and shakers and controlled the economy and the politics and all of this."],[1194.39,"And the the Greek speakers, the locals, previous to the Romans were an underclass of slaves and and servants and um it was very stratified."],[1207.65,"And there was there was uh the the Jewish community fairly small and insignificant."],[1217.69,"Um St. Paul's first visit to Philippi was in the book of Acts."],[1223.69,"We talked about a bunch of things, stories you probably remember, uh happened in Philippi, even if you don't remember they happened in Philippi."],[1231.93,"So Philippi was one of the first places he went when St."],[1235.13,"Remember, Saint Paul had the vision in the in the Acts of the Apostles that uh caused him to go to Macedonia uh to preach the gospel."],[1244.81,"And that was sort of the beginning of a new phase of his ministry."],[1248.77,"Because remember, before that, he had been going to cities with large synagogue communities."],[1252.97,"He'd go to the synagogue first"],[1255.3,"He was primarily going to these Jewish communities, and then, of course, then also preaching the gospel to those outside of the Jewish community."],[1263.86,"But he was using the synagogues as sort of a hub."],[1267.38,"Whereas when he goes into Macedonia, he's going to pagan cities with only small Jewish populations."],[1276.5,"So that was sort of a new phase that opened up."],[1280.1,"And Philippi is one of the first places he went in in Macedonia."],[1284.9,"So the things you might remember but not remember were in Philippi were first that's where St."],[1290.74,"Lydia was. Saint Lydia, who uh became a deaconess, uh, who was a dyer of purple fabric, um was there."],[1302.62,"She had been, she was not Jewish, she had been a God fearer or proselyte, as we've talked about before."],[1309.1,"She was one of these Gentiles who's sort of interested in Judaism, was a benefactor of the Jewish community, was around the Jewish community, but hadn't joined."],[1319.4,"So for her to join, uh her husband would have had to join, or she would have had to marry a Jewish man."],[1328.2,"Because that the obviously she couldn't be circumcised herself and become a become a proselyte."],[1334.44,"But so she was around the fringes of the Jewish community."],[1337.28,"She became a Christian and sort of became a big part of the foundation of the Christian community there in Philippi."],[1341.88,"of the Philippi is also where Saint Paul cast the demon out slave girl who was telling fortunes, whose owners were using her to make a living because she was possessed by this demon that would tell the future."],[1356.96,"And Saint Paul cast the demon out of her and ruined their business."],[1361.68,"And so they got mad and reported him, and St."],[1364.08,"Paul got thrown in jail in Philippi."],[1367.12,"And that's and it was in the jail in Philippi when they were chained to the floor and they were singing hymns and the earthquake happened."],[1374.88,"And the jailer was gonna kill himself."],[1377.6,"Remember, because he'd be executed for dereliction of duty if his prisoners escaped."],[1383.22,"By the Romans."],[1384.26,"Paul reassured him that nobody had actually fled, and that get then St"],[1384.46,"Um, but St."],[1390.62,"Paul used that as an opportunity to preach the gospel to him, and the the jailer and his family, who were Gentiles converted, became part of the Christian community."],[1400.14,"Um all of that happened in Philippi."],[1405.02,"So Philippi was not only a significant sort of Roman city, it was not only sort of a unique city in terms of St."],[1413.98,"When St. Paul went there, it was a transition and was different culturally than a lot of the other cities he preached in."],[1421.26,"Um, but it's also a city where a lot happened in terms of St."],[1425.5,"Paul's St. Paul's ministry."],[1427.26,"So it's significant in his ministry and just significant in in general."],[1431.66,"Yes, sir And so this is the community that St."],[1436.02,"Paul's now writing to."],[1438.42,"Was it a large important city?"],[1440.18,"Because I don't remember it being prominent in ancient history."],[1443.94,"It's not super prominent in that regard."],[1448.74,"The gold and silver mines were still there."],[1450.66,"So it was a city with a fair amount of wealth."],[1453.46,"Um but it wasn't a and those battles the battle happened near there."],[1459.7,"Um but it was sort of like I mean a battle happening near your city, it's sort of like Gettysburg."],[1464.58,"Yeah. Like Gettysburg, Pennsylvania is primarily significant because of the battle that happened near there, not because it's a hub of industry or something."],[1474.06,"Um, and so it was the same. kind of thing in that regard"],[1478.02,"So it was not an insane, you know, an insignificant city, it wasn't like a village, but it was definitely not Ephesus."],[1485.86,"Was it the capital of the province?"],[1488.26,"No. Okay. It was not Ephesus, it was not Corinth."],[1493.23,"It was not that level of significance."],[1496.11,"In fact, it had um when St."],[1499.63,"Paul visited and when this letter is coming to it, there were about 10,000 people living there."],[1505.87,"Uh to give you a comparison at this time, we're gonna read 1st and second Thessalonians in not too long."],[1512.11,"Uh Thessalonica or Thessaly, Thessalonica at that time, Thessaloniki, had uh about a hundred thousand, had about ten times as many. at this time"],[1522.39,"Um, so that gives you an idea of relative size."],[1526.92,"Um, so yeah, it's primarily it's a Roman settlement."],[1532.2,"It's very distinctly and proudly Roman, but not huge."],[1538.52,"Um, so this Christian community is uh almost entirely Gentile."],[1546.54,"Almost entirely former pagans or former even the Godfears, the people who had been attached to the Jewish community, even they had been pagans at some point."],[1557.82,"So really they're all former, almost all former pagans."],[1560.86,"Some of them had that intermediate step of being interested in Judaism."],[1566.02,"Uh, we've talked before about how there was sort of a Judaism fad among the Romans uh in the in the first century, and that's why there are all these Godfearers and all these non Jews kind of at the periphery of synagogues in different Roman cities."],[1583.06,"They were fascinated with Judaism for the same for some of the same reasons they were fascinated with Egyptian religion, just it was an ancient religious tradition that was kind of foreign and exotic."],[1593.38,"Um doubly so in the case of uh various forms of Judaism, because there were these sort of weird things, like that they only had one temple in one place, and that they didn't have idols, and the story. had circulated"],[1610.86,"I know we've mentioned this before, but when Pompey, the Roman general, annexed Judea to the Roman Empire."],[1618.62,"At the time he went down there, he forced his way into the Holy of Holies in the temple, and there was nothing there."],[1624.8,"The Ark was already gone."],[1625.84,"So he went there and it was just an empty, you know, there's this big curtain concealing."],[1629.76,"I gotta know what's behind this curtain."],[1631.52,"What have they got in their temple?"],[1633.2,"They've got to have some statue or some idol or some relic or some holy thing, and he barged back in there, there was an empty room."],[1641.04,"And uh so the rumor went around from that that that the uh the Judeans, the Jewish people, uh, worshipped their god Solamenteis only with their minds."],[1653.76,"And so this was you know especially to philosophically minded Greeks and Romans this was oh this is some kind of higher thing classic Roman Catholics yeah so they're they were they're kind of fascinated right by by it um not fascinated enough to get circumcised and abandon being Romans and become Jews and all that right not they're not going to go that far but fascinated enough that they'd want to have a copy of the Jewish scriptures in Greek and read it and be sort of interested and fascinated by it and discuss it, debate it."],[1690.96,"And they'd want to give some money to the local synagogue and get their name on a you know plaque that they you know were were uh uh the sort of magnanimous donors right to this this community and that kind of thing."],[1706.4,"Um and they knew that meant you know, hey, if we give them some money, not only do we get our name on the plaque, but uh they'll they'll pray for us to their god, and that can't hurt."],[1717.42,"You know, if you're a if you're a Roman pagan, every little bit helps with you know praying to different gods."],[1723.42,"So they were on board with that."],[1725.74,"Um so there were there were a fair number of those, like St."],[1728.38,"Lydia, obviously, being an example."],[1730.54,"um Um and then just direct, like the Philippian jailer, converts directly from paganism, uh, but very few uh Judean Christians."],[1743.86,"Because the Jewish communist uh you can imagine if there's like 10,000 people in the whole city."],[1749.47,"The Jewish community is pretty small."],[1751.55,"Um Lydia and the jailer might well have been among the recipients."],[1756.35,"Yes. Yeah. Yeah."],[1759.55,"Um St. Lydia particularly since she was serving as a deaconess."],[1764.32,"Um, so yeah, so this letter is coming to that same church."],[1769.44,"These are again people who St."],[1770.96,"Paul knew because he was there."],[1772.72,"Probably some more people had joined the Christian community since he left."],[1777.28,"But I mean, this is a community he's familiar with."],[1779.52,"Unlike, as we talked about in his epistle to the Romans, he hadn't been to Rome yet."],[1784.96,"So he was writing to people he didn't know."],[1788.2,"This is an epistle that's going primarily to people he knows and probably some other people who he knows of but hadn't met yet."],[1796.36,"But this is a community he's familiar with."],[1799.64,"He hadn't spent, though, on the other hand, he hadn't spent as much time there as he had in like Corinth or Ephesus."],[1807.34,"Where he spent years spending time with the people."],[1811.1,"So it's again, it's somewhere in between."],[1813.44,"Those two extremes of being very familiar, having lived there for years on the one hand, or not having met them yet on the other."],[1822.4,"These are people he's met, he's familiar with, some of whom he would have baptized, some of whom would have joined the community under his preaching, and then probably some others who were more recent."],[1832.16,"So another way in which St"],[1837.88,"Paul's epistle to the Philippians is a little different than some of the epistles we've been reading is that as we're gonna see, uh St."],[1848.12,"Paul's epistle to Philippians is gonna be much more positive than a lot of the uh the epistles we've been reading."],[1857.5,"So we've had epistles like Romans and Galatians that are sort of written directed at a particular problem."],[1865.63,"There was a problem in the churches of Galatia."],[1868.53,"These were these false preachers preaching another gospel."],[1872.05,"Uh we're gonna get a reference to them, actually, in Philippians. in terms of, hey, if any of those guys show up from to cause trouble"],[1882.81,"But so Galatians direct this problem."],[1884.81,"Romans, remember, we talked about the expulsion of the Jews and St."],[1888.89,"Paul trying to reconcile the Jewish Christian and Gentile Christian or formerly pagan Christian communities to make sure they stayed one community and didn't split in two."],[1900.97,"So they're directed at this particular problem."],[1903.77,"We've also read like first and second Corinthians, which are directed at like a whole bunch of problems."],[1909.63,"Because the community's just in crisis."],[1911.95,"There's tons of problematic things going on."],[1917.07,"While the community of Philippi, we're gonna see isn't perfect."],[1922.49,"We're gonna see St."],[1923.37,"Paul's able to write a much more positive on the whole epistle to them."],[1927.93,"Things are going a little better, at least so far."],[1932.09,"There. Remember, St."],[1934.57,"Paul said several times in 1st and 2 Corinthians how much he wished he could write to them a more positive letter, but he couldn't."],[1942.57,"Well, this is this is the kind of letter he was wanting to write to Corinth, but couldn't."],[1948.03,"Nothing's ever perfect."],[1949.55,"There's always dangers, there's always issues, but Philippi seems to have been doing better."],[1955.23,"And so St."],[1955.95,"Paul is able to write more positively."],[1959.63,"And uh so that means that that this epistle is gonna be a little more directed toward teaching and building up rather than addressing problems when we compare it to some of the others."],[1975.99,"And that's sort of important, important background to have, because we can when we're when we're reading different books of the Bible, it's very easy to sort of fall into a rut."],[1986.88,"So we're reading St."],[1988.08,"Paul's epistles, and we read like Romans and 1st and 2nd Corinthians and Galatians, and we say, okay, so St."],[1993.44,"Paul's writing these epistles to solve problems."],[1997.18,"And so as we read it, and this is helpful, like in 1 Corinthians, okay, what's the problem that St."],[2002.38,"Paul is addressing?"],[2004.8,"And that can even help us interpret sometimes."],[2007.04,"because Because for example, someone will. come along and pose an interpretation, say, oh, this is what it means"],[2013.48,"But if you think about it for five seconds, you're like, why would anyone in Corinth be teaching XYZ?"],[2023.21,"Uh, or in in Romans, we talked about how I hate to keep picking on the Calvinists after all the picking on Calvinists I did in Ephesians."],[2031.85,"Uh not really, I'm being dishonest."],[2035.61,"But anyway, I don't hate to pick on them."],[2038.33,"Sorry, not sorry."],[2039.45,"Um, but um, you know, when you get to when you get to Romans 9, and they're going to say, oh, well, this is all about how God chose some people for salvation and some people for damnation. before he created any of them"],[2055.41,"And you're sort of like, what does that have to do with chapter 8?"],[2061.41,"And why would he be telling the Romans that?"],[2066.69,"Like, what problem would that solve?"],[2071.99,"It doesn't make any sense."],[2073.19,"So that could be a helpful approach."],[2076.03,"But you could also fall into a position where you have a hammer, and so everything starts to look like a nail."],[2082.13,"And so you say, okay, this is how to read Saint Paul."],[2086.07,"And so if you come to St."],[2087.67,"Paul's epistle to the Philippians and try to approach it with, okay, everything he says is addressed to some kind of problem in Philippi."],[2094.95,"Because there's a different"],[2095.79,"You're going to get crazy results."],[2100.17,"And we'll see as we get into chapter one already, there's a different tone."],[2104.75,"And so it's written for different purposes."],[2107.48,"So you've got to, just like every letter you've written and every email you sent, you've sent in your life, has not had the same tone."],[2116.14,"Sometimes it's happy, sometimes it's sad, sometimes you're angry."],[2119.44,"Sometimes. And if you interpret all of them as you're angry."],[2127.18,"If everyone who read your emails interpreted every single one of them as you being angry, you wouldn't have a lot of friends left."],[2133.71,"So we have to take each each epistle."],[2136.83,"That's why I spend the time doing these introductions for each one"],[2142.04,"We have to take who's receiving it, what's going on there, what's it addressing, and then that affects how we read it."],[2149.4,"And so we're gonna be taking a little different approach in that regard to St."],[2154.92,"Paul's epistle to the Philippians, but that's not based on me being inconsistent."],[2160.18,"Or just we randomly choose to interpret different books of the Bible differently."],[2164.74,"It's based on the book itself and who's receiving it, and when it was it's based on context."],[2171.48,"So we have to take e eachach la thing St."],[2174.04,"Paul writes in the context he writes it, who he's writing it to, what he's writing it about, and that. should influence how we interpret it"],[2183.28,"And so, yes, each letter he writes, we should interpret a little bit differently, because he's not writing it to the same people at the same time to address the same problems."],[2193.52,"Um that's the end of our introduction."]],"o":[{"t":0.24,"lv":2,"h":"New book: Philippians","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Starting a new book tonight - <b>Philippians</b> (he slips: \"Corinthians, or not Corinthians\" - \"confused everyone\")</li><li class=\"d0\">Most of tonight = his \"long rambly <b>introduction</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Just finished <b>Ephesians</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Philippians \"a little different\" from the other Pauline epistles read so far</li><li class=\"d0\">Some basics the same: <b>St. Paul wrote it</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Back in the <b>Romans</b> intro: what \"Paul wrote it\" means</li><li class=\"d1\">not <b>pen and papyrus</b> or vellum, by hand</li><li class=\"d1\">true of <b>zero</b> of his epistles</li><li class=\"d1\">clue: \"see with what <b>large letters</b> I write to you in my own hand\" -> rest was not</li><li class=\"d1\">he affixes a <b>personal note or signature</b> at the end of some</li><li class=\"d0\">What is true: Paul was <b>involved in the writing of all of them</b></li></ul>"},{"t":117.9,"lv":3,"h":"One end of the spectrum: the scribe","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Spectrum = <b>how many factors</b> between Paul and the finished product</li><li class=\"d0\">One end: <b>Galatians, Romans</b>, 1 Corinthians (of those read)</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul <b>dictated</b> to an <i>amanuensis</i> = secretary, professional <b>scribe</b></li><li class=\"d1\">scribe wrote it, made little <b>grammar corrections</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"polish it up for written instead of spoken\" - like taking dictation</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul then <b>read it</b>, made any corrections (\"I didn't mean that word, I meant this word\")</li><li class=\"d0\">Scribe <b>finalized</b> it</li><li class=\"d0\">Same scribe usually <b>carried it</b> to the recipients and <b>read it publicly</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not everyone literate; <b>no quick way to make copies</b> - \"didn't put it in the bulletin\"</li><li class=\"d1\">scribe expected to <b>answer questions</b> about it</li><li class=\"d0\">Letters <b>first read publicly in the churches</b> from the get-go</li><li class=\"d1\">reading them in <b>liturgy</b> is not new - that is what they were for</li></ul>"},{"t":252.59,"lv":2,"h":"Philippians in the middle: Timothy and Hebrews","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Philippians sits <b>in the middle</b> of the spectrum</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>co-author</b> already in <b>1:1</b>: \"<b>Paul and Timothy</b>, bond servants (plural) of Jesus Christ\"</li><li class=\"d1\">so Paul + <b>Timothy</b> + a scribe all involved</li><li class=\"d0\">Other end: <b>Hebrews</b> - less direct, Paul's involvement \"isn't totally clear\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Greek style looks \"a lot more like <b>St. Luke</b> than St. Paul\"</li><li class=\"d1\">features look like a <b>sermon or homily</b> more than a letter - more at Hebrews</li><li class=\"d1\">theory: <b>Luke recorded a sermon</b> Paul gave, attached the letter stuff, sent it to a church</li><li class=\"d1\">text came from <b>Paul's mind</b>, but \"some distance\" between him and the text</li><li class=\"d0\">That is \"the range\"</li></ul>"},{"t":420.67,"lv":3,"h":"Authorship debates are fruitless","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Why repeat: <b>debates</b> - \"people need to publish journal articles\", write dissertations</li><li class=\"d0\">Any NT book: Paul \"definitely didn't write Hebrews, probably didn't write a bunch of the others\"</li><li class=\"d1\">usually <b>about seven</b> \"definitely\" his - <b>Romans, Galatians</b> end of the spectrum</li><li class=\"d1\">Hebrews definitely not at the other end; <b>the rest debatable</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Answer: most \"debatable\" ones have a <b>listed co-author</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Hebrews written in style of <b>one of Paul's companions</b></li><li class=\"d1\">so they don't all look alike in the Greek</li><li class=\"d0\">Church read the <b>original Greek</b>, even more aware of style differences than we are - still held they come from <b>St. Paul</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"Kind of <b>fruitless</b>\" to argue - unless you must write a dissertation</li><li class=\"d1\">no, he didn't sit down and write any of it</li><li class=\"d1\">but clear <b>connection to Paul</b> - ideas \"quote unquote <b>Pauline</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Irrelevant to <b>interpreting</b> and to the <b>tradition of the church</b></li><li class=\"d1\">this body of literature <b>reflects the mind of St. Paul</b>; \"the gospel as Paul proclaimed it\"</li><li class=\"d1\">none put to paper by him - \"no one ever claimed it was\"</li></ul>"},{"t":567.97,"lv":2,"h":"Where and when: Ephesus, mid-50s","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Epistles with <b>Timothy as co-author</b> all date from <b>around the same period</b></li><li class=\"d1\">good argument they originate from <b>Ephesus</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Paul alludes to <b>trouble he had in Ephesus</b>; Ephesus a major center</li><li class=\"d1\">Timothy in Ephesus until Paul sent him to <b>Crete</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Likely like Ephesians: written <b>mid-50s AD</b>, from Ephesus, <b>while in prison</b></li><li class=\"d1\">why a co-author: Paul \"<b>less free to compose</b> as he usually composed\" -> Timothy assisted</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Older theory</b>: written from <b>Rome</b> imprisonment</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Orthodox Study Bible</b> frontispiece has this (\"almost willing to bet\")</li><li class=\"d0\">Problems with Rome:</li><li class=\"d1\">nothing says <b>Timothy ever went to Rome</b>; he stayed at Ephesus</li><li class=\"d1\">why Paul wrote <b>1 and 2 Timothy</b> from Rome - if Timothy were with him, no letters needed</li><li class=\"d1\">plans Paul mentions, esp. at <b>end of Philippians</b>, make no sense from Rome - things he'd <b>already done</b></li></ul>"},{"t":732.93,"lv":3,"h":"An earlier epistle, mid-career","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">More likely: <b>same imprisonment in Ephesus</b>, which was <b>temporary</b></li><li class=\"d0\">So one of his <b>earlier</b> epistles</li><li class=\"d1\">all epistles written between ~<b>45 AD</b> and the <b>60s</b>, when <b>Nero</b> martyred him</li><li class=\"d1\">\"not a huge span\" - about <b>20 years</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Philippians \"<b>smack in the middle</b>\" of his ministry and writing career</li></ul>"},{"t":779.15,"lv":2,"h":"Written to one particular city","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Types of recipients so far:</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Romans, 1 and 2 Corinthians</b>: one community, one city</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Galatians, Ephesians</b>: more <b>circular</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Galatia</b> = a region -> all the churches there</li><li class=\"d1\">Ephesians written from Ephesus, circulated among communities <b>around Ephesus</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Philippians</b>: one particular city - <b>Philippi</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"a bunch of Philippis\" - e.g. <b>Caesarea Philippi</b></li><li class=\"d1\">this one in the Roman province of <b>Macedonia</b></li><li class=\"d1\">also <b>Alexander the Great's Macedonia</b> (\"of course he was Macedonian\")</li></ul>"},{"t":865.01,"lv":2,"h":"History of Philippi: Philip of Macedon","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Admits: \"<b>not huge fans</b> of Philippi, probably\" - hasn't done a ton of research on it</li><li class=\"d0\">First incorporated as <b>Greek city-state</b> by <b>Philip of Macedon</b>, Alexander's father</li><li class=\"d1\">named it after himself \"in a fit of humility\" - his son did that a lot too</li><li class=\"d1\">people already settled there</li><li class=\"d1\">why a city-state: <b>gold and silver mines</b> close by</li><li class=\"d1\">city gave him the <b>infrastructure</b> to extract them</li><li class=\"d1\">also near the <b>border</b> of territory he had recently <b>annexed</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Greek start; much more prominent in <b>Roman period</b></li></ul>"},{"t":928.33,"lv":3,"h":"The Roman colony","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>42 BC</b>: armies of <b>Octavian</b> (later <b>Caesar Augustus</b>) and <b>Mark Antony</b> - \"back when they were friends\"</li><li class=\"d1\">finally tracked down <b>Cassius and Brutus</b>, who killed Caesar</li><li class=\"d1\">final showdown battle <b>near Philippi</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Reward to victorious <b>veterans</b>: how the Roman system worked</li><li class=\"d1\">end of service = <b>land</b>, usually at the <b>edges of Roman territory</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Romans settle, \"<b>Romanize</b>,\" bring the Roman way of life</li><li class=\"d1\">solidifies the empire: natives \"not necessarily fond of Rome\" -> bring in <b>Roman settlers</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Octavian settled a wave of loyal veterans around Philippi</li><li class=\"d1\">\"another fit of humility\" - <b>renamed the city</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Bible says Philippi, but technically <i>Iulio Augusto Philippi</i></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Julius Caesar</b>, \"illustrious ancestor\" + <b>Augustus</b> (name he'd just taken) + Philip of Macedon's name on the end</li></ul>"},{"t":1072.64,"lv":3,"h":"A very Roman city; Q&amp;A: where is it","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Philippi = \"a <b>very Roman</b> city\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Contrast: other cities (Asia Minor, Greece, Palestine, Syria, <b>Antioch</b>, Egypt)</li><li class=\"d1\">inscriptions and documents in <b>Greek</b> - the <b>lingua franca</b>, trade language</li><li class=\"d0\">Philippi: almost all first-century inscriptions in <b>Latin</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"everybody knew Greek, but <b>only Romans knew Latin</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">shows the share of the population that was <b>self-consciously Roman</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Q: Where is Philippi today - Turkey, Bulgaria?</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>northeastern Greece</b></li><li class=\"d1\">what's called \"Macedonia\" today is <b>not ancient Macedonia</b> - Greeks \"very particular\" about pointing that out</li><li class=\"d1\">ancient Macedonia is part of <b>Greece</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Strict class system</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Roman citizens, settlers, veterans = \"movers and shakers\" - <b>economy and politics</b></li><li class=\"d1\">local Greek speakers = <b>underclass</b> of slaves and servants; \"very stratified\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Jewish community</b> there \"fairly small and insignificant\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1217.69,"lv":2,"h":"Paul's first visit: a new phase","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">First visit in <b>Acts</b> - stories you may remember without remembering they were at Philippi</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul's <b>vision</b> sent him to <b>Macedonia</b> to preach</li><li class=\"d1\">Philippi one of the first places - start of a <b>new phase</b> of his ministry</li><li class=\"d0\">Before: cities with <b>large synagogue communities</b></li><li class=\"d1\">went to the <b>synagogue first</b>; Jewish communities as a <b>hub</b>, then preached to those outside</li><li class=\"d0\">Macedonia: <b>pagan cities</b> with only small Jewish populations</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>St. Lydia</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not Jewish; a <b>Godfearer</b> or proselyte - Gentile interested in Judaism, a <b>benefactor</b> of the community</li><li class=\"d1\">to join, her <b>husband</b> would have had to join, or she'd marry a Jewish man - \"obviously she couldn't be circumcised\"</li><li class=\"d1\">on the fringes -> became a Christian, big part of the <b>foundation</b> of the church there</li></ul>"},{"t":1341.88,"lv":3,"h":"The slave girl and the jailer","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Paul cast the <b>demon</b> out of the <b>slave girl</b> who told fortunes</li><li class=\"d1\">owners made a living from her - Paul \"<b>ruined their business</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">they got mad, reported him -> Paul <b>thrown in jail</b> in Philippi</li><li class=\"d0\">In the jail: <b>chained to the floor</b>, singing hymns, <b>earthquake</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>jailer</b> about to kill himself - executed for <b>dereliction of duty</b> if prisoners escaped</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul reassured him: nobody had fled</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul preached the gospel to him; jailer and family, <b>Gentiles</b>, converted -> part of the church</li><li class=\"d0\">Philippi: Roman city, <b>transition point</b>, culturally different from other cities - and \"a lot happened\" in Paul's ministry</li><li class=\"d0\">Significant in his ministry and in general</li><li class=\"d0\">This is the community Paul is now writing to</li></ul>"},{"t":1438.42,"lv":3,"h":"Q&amp;A: how important was Philippi?","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: Large, important city? Don't remember it prominent in ancient history</li><li class=\"d1\">not super prominent</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>gold and silver mines</b> still there -> \"a fair amount of <b>wealth</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">battle happened near there - like <b>Gettysburg</b>: significant for the battle, not as a hub of industry</li><li class=\"d0\">Not insignificant, not a village - \"definitely not <b>Ephesus</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Q: Capital of the province? - <b>No</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not Ephesus, not <b>Corinth</b> - \"not that level\"</li><li class=\"d0\">About <b>10,000 people</b> when Paul visited and wrote</li><li class=\"d1\">comparison: <b>Thessalonica</b> ~<b>100,000</b>, ten times as many - we'll read 1 and 2 Thessalonians \"not too long\"</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Distinctly and <b>proudly Roman</b>, but not huge\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1538.52,"lv":2,"h":"An almost entirely Gentile church","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Church \"almost entirely <b>Gentile</b>\" - former <b>pagans</b></li><li class=\"d1\">even the <b>Godfearers</b> had been pagans at some point</li><li class=\"d1\">some had the intermediate step of interest in <b>Judaism</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Judaism a <b>fad among the Romans</b> in the first century -> Godfearers at the periphery of synagogues</li><li class=\"d1\">fascinated for same reasons as <b>Egyptian religion</b>: ancient, foreign, <b>exotic</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"doubly so\" with Judaism: <b>one temple in one place</b>, <b>no idols</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1610.86,"lv":3,"h":"Pompey and the empty Holy of Holies","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Story circulated: <b>Pompey</b>, general who annexed <b>Judea</b> to Rome</li><li class=\"d1\">forced his way into the <b>Holy of Holies</b>: \"I gotta know what's behind this <b>curtain</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">expected a statue, idol, relic - found an <b>empty room</b> (the <b>Ark</b> already gone)</li><li class=\"d0\">Rumor: the Judeans \"worshipped their god <b>only with their minds</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">to philosophically minded Greeks and Romans: \"some kind of <b>higher thing</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Fascinated, but not enough to get <b>circumcised</b> and abandon being Romans - \"not going to go that far\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Enough to:</li><li class=\"d1\">want the <b>Scriptures in Greek</b>, read, discuss, debate</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>give money to the synagogue</b>, get name on a <b>plaque</b> as \"magnanimous donors\"</li><li class=\"d1\">know the Jews would <b>pray for them to their god</b> - \"can't hurt\"; for a Roman pagan \"every little bit helps\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1725.74,"lv":3,"h":"Few Judean Christians; Lydia and the jailer","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">A fair number of Godfearers - <b>St. Lydia</b> an example</li><li class=\"d0\">Others straight from <b>paganism</b> - the <b>Philippian jailer</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Very few <b>Judean Christians</b> - city of ~10,000, <b>Jewish community small</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Lydia and the jailer might well have been <b>among the recipients</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Lydia especially, since she served as a <b>deaconess</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Same church Paul knew</li></ul>"},{"t":1779.52,"lv":2,"h":"A community Paul knows","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Contrast with <b>Romans</b>: he hadn't been to Rome yet -> writing to people he <b>didn't know</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Philippians: primarily people he <b>knows</b>, plus some he knows of but hasn't met</li><li class=\"d0\">Not as much time as in <b>Corinth or Ephesus</b> - \"<b>years</b> with the people\"</li><li class=\"d0\">So \"somewhere in between\" two extremes:</li><li class=\"d1\">very familiar, lived there for years</li><li class=\"d1\">not met them yet</li><li class=\"d0\">These he's met</li><li class=\"d1\">some he'd have <b>baptized</b></li><li class=\"d1\">some joined under his <b>preaching</b></li><li class=\"d1\">probably others more <b>recent</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1832.16,"lv":2,"h":"A more positive letter","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Another difference: much <b>more positive</b> than many epistles read</li><li class=\"d0\">Earlier ones aimed at a <b>problem</b>:</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Galatians</b>: <b>false preachers</b> preaching \"another gospel\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Philippians will refer to them - \"if any of those guys show up to cause trouble\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Romans</b>: <b>expulsion of the Jews</b>; reconciling Jewish Christian and Gentile Christian communities so they stayed <b>one community</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>1 and 2 Corinthians</b>: \"a whole bunch of problems\" - community \"in crisis\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Philippi \"isn't perfect,\" but Paul can write a <b>much more positive</b> epistle</li><li class=\"d1\">\"things are going a little better, at least so far\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul said several times in 1 and 2 Corinthians he <b>wished he could write a more positive letter</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Philippians = \"the kind of letter he was wanting to write to Corinth, but couldn't\"</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Nothing's ever perfect\" - but Philippi doing better</li><li class=\"d0\">So more directed toward <b>teaching and building up</b> than addressing problems</li></ul>"},{"t":1975.99,"lv":2,"h":"Danger of the problem-solving rut","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Reading different books: easy to fall into a <b>rut</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Habit: \"St. Paul writes epistles to <b>solve problems</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">helpful in <b>1 Corinthians</b>: what problem is he addressing?</li><li class=\"d1\">can help <b>interpret</b>: someone proposes a reading -> \"why would anyone in Corinth be teaching XYZ?\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Example: <b>Romans 9</b> and the <b>Calvinists</b> (\"sorry, not sorry\")</li><li class=\"d1\">claim: God chose some for salvation, some for damnation before creating them</li><li class=\"d1\">\"what does that have to do with <b>chapter 8</b>?\" Why tell the Romans that? What problem would it solve?</li><li class=\"d1\">\"it doesn't make any sense\"</li><li class=\"d0\">But: \"you have a hammer, so everything looks like a <b>nail</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">read Philippians as everything addressed to a problem in Philippi -> \"<b>crazy results</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2100.17,"lv":3,"h":"Tone and context","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Already in chapter 1 there's a <b>different tone</b> - written for <b>different purposes</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Like every <b>letter or email</b> you've sent - not the same tone</li><li class=\"d1\">happy, sad, angry</li><li class=\"d1\">if everyone read all your emails as angry -> \"you wouldn't have a lot of friends left\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Why he does an <b>introduction</b> for each epistle:</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>who</b> is receiving it, <b>what's going on</b>, what it's addressing -> affects how we read</li><li class=\"d0\">A little different approach for Philippians - \"not based on me being <b>inconsistent</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">not \"randomly choosing\" to interpret books differently</li><li class=\"d1\">based on the <b>book itself</b>, who receives it, when - <b>context</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Read each thing Paul writes in <b>its context</b>: who, about what</li><li class=\"d1\">each letter interpreted a little differently - not the same <b>people, time, problems</b></li><li class=\"d0\">End of introduction</li></ul>"}]});
